  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington had a rough Wednesday\u2014down 21% total revenue with only 5 groomer-days on the floor, producing $466 per groomer against a baseline of $478. Transaction count collapsed 42% day-over-day, suggesting a booking/scheduling gap rather than conversion weakness; add-on attach actually improved to 46% from 41%, so upsell discipline held. The real story is capacity: we ran lean yesterday and it showed. The good news is month-to-date Port is up 9% real performance (7.3% above DOW-adjusted expectation), appointments up 14%, and year-to-date revenue is up 45%\u2014that's structural growth. Hicksville fired yesterday: up 33% total, up 29% grooming, with 4 groomer-days producing $449 (5.9% above trailing baseline). Retail nearly doubled. Month-to-date Hicksville is outperforming expectations by 18.7% real growth, up 17% total and 19% in appointments. Both stores show improving per-groomer productivity\u2014Port at $463 MTD and Hicksville at $432 MTD\u2014but Port's overall MTD $/groomer-day is down 6.5% from last month despite higher groomer staffing, suggesting either booking density has thinned or scheduling efficiency needs tightening. The pattern: you're running more groomer-days but extracting less per head, which is workable only if volume scales further. Inventory pressure is real\u2014X-Small Treat Table is critically short at both locations (negative stock), and three other SKUs need immediate reorder. Port has 469 customers in the lapse pool worth nearly $297k annually; Hicksville has 119 worth $91k. Both are reactivation opportunities.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port has 6 groomer-days today and 7 tomorrow (7.0h bathers each); Hicksville has 4 today and 4 tomorrow (0.0h bathers); combined next 7 days: 56 groomer-days and 43.0h bathers across both stores."
